```

Hmm but this test does not currently read the log... it has `log` in scope. Yes it does (`const log = join(dir, "gh.log")`).

Rather than modifying that test (its subject is the recorded ending), maybe better to add a dedicated test: "create_pr's waiting comment names the agent, so a parent parked on a pull request can be re-invoked". That's clearer as a behaviour test. But fixtures are heavy (60s). Reusing the existing one with two extra assertions is cheaper and states the behaviour. Hmm, "A test says what" — the existing test's name is about recording; adding unrelated assertions muddies it. I'll add the assertions to the launch_sub_agent test (which is a park test anyway) and create one new focused test for create_pr? The fixture setup is ~10 lines; acceptable.

Hmm, let me reconsider: is create_pr's waiting comment ever on a node whose recovery depends on it? `create_pr` runs on an issue; the node's agent then waits for CI/reviewer. The aggregation gate re-invokes the *parent* of a closed sub-issue. If the node with the PR is itself a sub-issue of a parent, and the PR merges → the post-merge dispatch reads ORIGIN_AGENT_TAG from the PR body (works). But if the sub-issue gets closed by something else... Hmm. Also `/resume` on such a node needs the agent name. So yes, the create_pr park benefits too, and the issue names it.

I'll add a focused test in the mcp tests for create_pr's waiting comment carrying the agent. Where to put it — right after the "records the comment it ends the run on" test. It needs the same fixture. Let me write it.

Actually — simpler and better: the assertion in the existing launch test plus one new create_pr test. Fine.

Also, should I check the `dist/` generated workflow? No: tools are TS bundled.

Now, wait. One more important consideration about `postTurnComment`/`recordEndingComment` and `withoutBookkeeping`: `post_result_comment`'s `withoutBookkeeping` strips AGENT_TAG etc. when reading bodies back. Our waiting comments are read raw by mostRecentAgent (good).

Another consideration: `mostRecentAgent` reads AGENT_TAG.readProse. `proseOnly` strips fenced blocks. Our waiting body may include a `<details><summary>Report</summary>` with the agent's summary — if the summary contains a fenced block with an `atomaton:agent` tag, `proseOnly` removes it. Fine.

Important: the report inside the waiting comment could contain a `/engineer`-like line or a dispatch marker in prose! `mostRecentAgent` checks AGENT_TAG first in the same body — and AGENT_TAG is the header, so it wins. Good: the header tag is found first within the body. Yes: `AGENT_TAG.readProse(body)` searches the whole body, so the header tag matches first (regex finds the first match anywhere; the header is at the top). 

Hmm, but careful: what if the report body contains something that makes `isQuotation(body)` true? `isQuotation` uses `QUOTES_TAG.has(body)` (raw, not prose). If a summary quoted a previous notice... The waiting comment body is the agent's summary, which could contain an `atomaton:quotes=123` tag if it quoted machinery output. Then `mostRecentAgent` would skip this comment entirely. That's an unlikely but real hazard — and the same hazard applies to `post_result_comment`'s agent tag today (a result comment whose body quotes the guard's notice would be skipped). Hmm, actually `withoutBookkeeping`... no, `mostRecentAgent` reads raw bodies and skips quotations entirely. Existing behaviour; not my problem to fix, though worth noting? It's a pre-existing hazard that the fix inherits. Since the summary is the agent's own text and the machinery folds it into `<details>`, a quoted tag would be there. I'll not chase it; but let me consider putting the agent tag... no, can't avoid a whole-body quotation check.

Hmm, actually I could be smarter: `mostRecentAgent` could read the agent tag from the header even in a quotation... no, out of scope.

OK, now also: the `handoffsSincePerson` narrowing uses `isResultComment` which uses `isQuotation` — same skip semantics. Fine.

